We don't have Michael Shea here, but we do have an extra special, wonderful guest in the form of...
我们虽然没有请到迈克尔·谢伊，但我们有一位格外特别、非常棒的嘉宾……

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:53.620
Craig, introduce our special guest.
克雷格，介绍一下我们的特别嘉宾。

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.260
We have a Hymm All Star writer.
我们有一位“老妈”全明星编剧。

00:01:57.260 --> 00:01:59.640
This is his first episode, right?
这是他的第一集，对吧？

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.480
This is joined in season three and stayed a long time.
他是在第三季加入的，并且呆了很长时间。

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:13.960
We'll talk about the rest of the run of having this wonderful man, hilarious dude, has done so many cool things since How Much Mother 2, the great Joe Kelly, ladies and gentlemen.
我们会聊聊之后的故事，能请到这位风趣幽默、在《老爸老妈浪漫史》之后做过那么多酷炫事情的大帅哥，伟大乔·凯利，女士们先生们。

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:14.960
Joe Kelly.
乔·凯利。

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:15.960
JK.
JK。
JK。JK。

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:16.960
How are you guys?
你们好吗？

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:17.960
Good to be.
很高兴来到这里。

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:18.960
So such a pleasure.
真是荣幸。

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:19.960
Thanks for joining us.
谢谢你加入我们。

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:20.960
Yeah, pleasure.
是的，荣幸。

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:21.960
Yeah.
是的。

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:29.159
Joe, so I'm trying to remember we, I think we had read your spec scripts like a couple, like the year before we hired you.
乔，我在努力回想，我想我们在雇你前的一两年，读过你的样片剧本。

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:31.860
I remember reading you and really liking your stuff.
我记得读过你的作品，非常喜欢。

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:33.699
And like the stars didn't align.
但当时机缘巧合没成。

00:02:33.699 --> 00:02:35.300
And then we got even season three.
然后到了第三季。

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:39.020
What were you doing right before you joined how much mother in season three?
在第三季加入《老爸老妈浪漫史》之前，你在做什么？

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:39.860
Was it SNL?
是《周六夜现场》吗？

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:41.539
I was trying to piece it together.
我一直在试图把这些记忆拼凑起来。

00:02:41.539 --> 00:02:44.180
Give us like a portrait of where you were like leading up to that.
给我们描绘一下你当时的情况吧。

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.090
I love the SNL and then I came to L.A.
我热爱《周六夜现场》，然后我来到了洛杉矶。

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:51.330
And I made, I was, Joel Gallon was paying my rent.
我当时在创作，乔尔·加伦在帮我付房租。

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.729
I was doing MTV movie awards.
我当时在主持MTV电影奖。

00:02:53.729 --> 00:02:55.729
I had been directed the first movie.
我刚执导了第一部电影。

00:02:55.729 --> 00:02:57.289
I was in another teen movie.
我参演了另一部青少年电影。

00:02:57.289 --> 00:02:59.210
I mean, we really loved that movie.
我是说，我们真的很喜欢那部电影。

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.270
I'm a huge, now the King movie guy.
我是一个超级大——现在成了斯蒂芬·金电影的粉丝。

00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.810
I remember that, I remember that.
我记得那个，我记得那个。

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:10.650
And like William Shatner, the roast of William Shatner, like I was just doing that.
还有像威廉·夏特纳，威廉·夏特纳的吐槽大会，我当时就在做那个。

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.830
And I remember meeting you guys and feeling great about it.
我记得见过你们，感觉非常棒。

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.550
And then no, he didn't hire me, it's a season two.
然后不，他没有雇我，那是第二季。

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.389
That's what I remember too.
这也是我记忆中的情景。

00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:21.169
I remember we really liked you, but we didn't hire you.
我记得我们真的很喜欢你，但我们没有录用你。

00:03:21.169 --> 00:03:22.389
You probably hit all your guts.
你当时可能恨透了。

00:03:22.389 --> 00:03:23.229
It worked out season three.
到了第三季结果就好了。

00:03:23.229 --> 00:03:24.729
But I came, we came around.
不过我来了，我们还是合作了。

00:03:24.729 --> 00:03:25.569
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:03:25.569 --> 00:03:29.050
Joe was, how I made your mother, your first staffing on a like an episodic show?
乔，《老爸老妈浪漫史》是你第一次加入 episodic（常规剧集）的编剧团队吗？

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:31.399
was and I was terrified.
是的，当时我吓坏了。

00:03:31.399 --> 00:03:33.161
I was the new guy.
我是新人。

00:03:33.161 --> 00:03:37.370
Everyone had been together for two seasons.
大家已经在一起合作了两季。

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:43.434
They all talked about this epic lightsaber fight they had in the parking lot one day.
他们总是在谈论有一天在停车场发生的一场史诗般的激光剑大战。

00:03:43.434 --> 00:03:47.858
I would never catch up to the glorious time they had that day.
我永远也赶不上他们那天度过的美好时光了。

00:03:47.858 --> 00:03:54.778
So slow and steady and every time I said something, Stephen Lloyd would wince and pinch his nose.
所以我只能慢下来，稳扎稳打，每一次我开口说话，斯蒂芬·劳埃德（Stephen Lloyd）就会畏缩并捏住鼻子。

00:03:54.778 --> 00:04:02.770
So my goal was just just to say something and not have Stephen Lloyd wince pinch his nose and put his head down.
所以我的目标仅仅是说点什么，而不要让斯蒂芬·劳埃德畏缩、捏鼻子并把头埋下去。

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.169
And I think I got there towards the end.
我想我在快结束时做到了这一点。

00:04:05.169 --> 00:04:06.930
Oh, you got there.
哦，你做到了。

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:07.770
You got there.
你做到了。

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:12.330
So you came from like a heavy like joke sketch background.
所以你背景比较偏向搞笑小品类。

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:18.129
Did you feel like comedically like you could hang and you had to learn more about story structure and character stuff?
你觉得自己在喜剧方面能应付自如吗？还是说你必须去学习更多关于故事结构和角色方面的东西？

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:22.329
Like where did you feel like you were your strengths and weaknesses were when you first joined?
你刚加入时，觉得自己的强项和弱点分别在哪里？

00:04:22.329 --> 00:04:25.449
Yes, I mean sketch in improv.
是的，我是说小品和即兴表演。

00:04:25.449 --> 00:04:28.670
And I was confident in those arenas.
我在这些领域很有自信。

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:40.470
And then I was good at pitching bids and how much mother with the pops was a great place for me to be, it melded the two very sketchy at times with the things we did.
而且我很擅长提出各种鬼点子，《老爸老妈浪漫史》对我来说是一个绝佳的地方，它把这两者融合在了一起，有时我们做的东西非常像小品。

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.310
It could be sketchy show, yeah.
确实可以算是个“小品式”的节目，对吧。

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.389
Yeah, in all sorts of ways, especially for the others.
对，各种意义上的，尤其是对其他人来说。

00:04:44.389 --> 00:04:46.149
It's sketchy in every sense of the world.
简直在各个方面都很“不靠谱/像小品”（sketchy）。

00:04:46.149 --> 00:04:47.509
The bracket is fairly sketchy.
这个括号其实挺可疑的。

00:04:47.509 --> 00:04:53.110
Yeah, but in five years I really learned the other half of the power of the show.
是的，但在五年里，我真正学到了这部剧力量的另一半。

00:04:53.110 --> 00:05:06.149
And I learned again and again, I would always get really excited about the bigger comedic ideas and the emotional ones were always what registered more to the audience and were better and the stuff that grounded.
我一次又一次地吸取教训，我总是会对那些宏大的喜剧点子感到兴奋，但实际上情感向的内容总是更能引起观众共鸣，效果更好，也更能落地。

00:05:06.149 --> 00:05:18.550
Every time I think I learned that lesson, I write a first draft of something and it's just all jokes that are 12 out of 10 on the meter and I just have to dial it down and dial it down.
每次我觉得自己吸取了这个教训时，当我写下某个东西的初稿，里面全都是刻度达到10分满分的12分笑话，然后我就不得不把它们一点点收敛、一点点调低。

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:21.709
But yeah, it was, it was.
但确实，就是这样。

00:05:21.709 --> 00:05:27.509
Well, you went on to co-create a show that is like really heart forward, right?
嗯，后来你联合创作了一部非常走心的剧，对吧？

00:05:27.509 --> 00:05:29.110
Like, do you feel...
比如，你觉得……

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:36.589
Joe created Ted Lasso, which is, I guess, the only works with Ted Protagonist, which I appreciate.
乔创作了《泰德·拉索》，我想这也是唯一一部以泰德为主角的剧集，我很欣赏这一点。

00:05:36.589 --> 00:05:38.430
Yeah, so many times.
对，太多次了。

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.750
You worked for the most famous Ted's in some kind of history.
你为某种历史中可以说是最著名的泰德们工作过。

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.589
But did you feel like by the time you...
但是，你觉得当你……

00:05:43.589 --> 00:05:45.230
Sorry.
抱歉。

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:54.670
But did you feel like by the time you left, talking about your mother, that you really had learned how to write in that way, or that you picked up that skill?
但是你觉得当你离开《老爸老妈浪漫史》的时候，你真的学会了那种写作方式，或者说你掌握了这项技能吗？

00:05:55.970 --> 00:06:12.209
Yeah, I mean, again, it took a while to understand, because the Ted Lasso started off with the commercials that were just a football coach in England, and when Jason said he wanted to do the show, I was like, that's gonna be a show, okay?
是的，我是说，这确实花了一些时间去理解，因为《泰德·拉索》一开始只是关于一个英格兰足球教练的广告，当杰森说他想把这拍成剧时，我想：“这也能成一部剧？好吧。”

00:06:12.209 --> 00:06:14.410
I can keep pitching those England bits.
我可以继续构思那些关于英格兰的段子。

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:21.449
And then I had no idea, it wasn't until editing, I understood because it was going to be a funnier show.
然后我完全不知道，直到剪辑时我才明白，因为本来它会是一个更好笑的节目。

00:06:21.449 --> 00:06:24.149
No, it's going to be a longer, more emotional show.
不，它会是一个更长、更有情感的节目。

00:06:24.149 --> 00:06:29.250
There was a push and pull up until the final edits of season one.
直到第一季的最终剪辑完成之前，一直存在着拉锯和博弈。

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:33.930
And then it was like, oh, okay, that's the tone we're landing on.
然后就好像是，哦，好吧，这就是我们最终定下的基调。

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.250
And it was much, again, much more sincere than I imagined going in.
而且它比我预想的要真诚得多。

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:41.970
Again, just pitching my fart and poop jokes and seeing what sticks to the wall.
话说回来，我也就是抛出我的放屁和便便笑话，看看哪个能行得通。

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:44.170
But you are very capable of those other speeds.
但你非常擅长其他那些节奏。

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:47.529
And I will say like, we needed your energy.
而且我要说的是，我们需要你的能量。

00:06:47.529 --> 00:06:53.191
needed you to come in with that energy because I think we were afraid that we were such an emo sitcom, right?
我们需要你带着那种能量加入，因为我想我们曾担心我们是一部过于忧郁的情景喜剧，对吧？

00:06:53.191 --> 00:06:54.594
And we knew we were on CBS.
而且我们知道我们在CBS播出。

00:06:54.594 --> 00:07:02.489
Like the biggest hit show was two and a half men on CBS and Aaron after How Much Mother and would have millions more viewers than us every Monday night.
就像CBS收视率最高的热门节目是《好汉两个半》，而卡特在《老爸老妈浪漫史》之后，每周一都会比我们多出数百万的观众。

00:07:02.489 --> 00:07:05.502
And we were really lived in like this inferiority to that.
我们真的活在那一种自卑感中。

00:07:05.502 --> 00:07:12.463
And we were always getting the note from CBS and like Les Moonves was like the president of CBS like super like macho old school dude.
我们总是收到CBS的意见，比如莱斯·穆恩维斯当时是CBS的总裁，一个超级大男子主义的老派家伙。

00:07:12.463 --> 00:07:17.449
Everyone kept saying like if you want to stay on the air maybe a a few more jokes for the dudes.
每个人都一直说，如果你想继续留在电视上，也许得为直男们多准备几个笑话。

00:07:17.449 --> 00:07:25.009
And that just takes the stuff to talk about the bracket, the March Madness-based episode, where Barney is in full sociopathic dudes mode.
这就引出了谈论那个对阵表的话题，也就是以疯狂三月为主题的那集，巴尼展现出完全反社会直男的状态。

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:28.930
But like, we wanted that energy.
不过，总之，我们需要那种能量。

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.930
You are very funny, Joe.
你非常搞笑，乔。

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.970
You were very, like, you came in very hot with jokes, with funny jokes.
你真的很……带着满满的能量、带着搞笑的段子加入。

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:40.850
You were on SNL, you had done Sketch, you had performed, you had performed improv, like you lived in Amsterdam, and like, you were on stage.
你曾在《周六夜生活》工作过，演过小品，表演过，表演过即兴喜剧，你还在阿姆斯特丹生活过，而且你上过舞台。

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.329
Like, we needed some of that energy.
所以，我们需要那种能量。

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:47.389
because we, if left door on devices, Carter and I are very emo.
因为如果我们任由自己发展，卡特和我其实非常多愁善感。

00:07:47.389 --> 00:07:53.389
You know, we are, we are, we are, we are writing like a humor list, essentially humor list.
你知道的，我们写的剧本基本上没什么幽默感，可以说毫无幽默感。

00:07:53.389 --> 00:07:59.870
I, we, look, I think we, Carter and I would always say like, we never worry too much about the jokes.
我……你看，我想我们——卡特和我总是会说，我们从不过多担心笑话。

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:09.350
We always want an interesting structure to know what it's emotionally about and then come in and someone like machine gun us some jokes and we, you were, you brought that energy, Joe.
我们总是想要一个有趣的结构，来弄清楚它在情感上讲的是什么，然后让某人像机关枪一样朝我们突突一堆笑话，而你，乔，你带来了那种能量。

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:14.269
And then I think over the seasons that you were there, We are Emonus rubbed off on you a little bit.
然后我觉得在你待在那里的那几季里，我们多愁善感的特质也多多少少影响了你一点。

00:08:14.269 --> 00:08:20.350
And I think you also steered us more towards like, hey, remember, there's some men that watch how I met your mother too.
而且我想你也引导我们更多地去想，嘿，记住，也有一些男人在看《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.350
And they might like things like sports.
他们可能会喜欢体育之类的事情。

00:08:23.389 --> 00:08:26.790
And so it was, I don't know, I think it was a good match.
所以，怎么说呢，我觉得这是一次很好的搭配。

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.430
I think we were a good match for one another, I feel.
我觉得我们彼此配合得很好，我是这么觉得的。

00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:31.990
Yeah, I know it was the same for me.
是的，我知道我的感受也一样。

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:36.549
I also was like, Jason would always say, let's break the story of a drama.
我也觉得，杰森总是会说，让我们先把一个剧本的剧情结构搭好。

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:38.789
And then the jokes will just add afterwards.
然后笑话就可以随后再加上去。

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.710
So as much as the story works on an emotional level.
只要这个故事在情感层面成立就行。

00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:57.029
And then when we broke those stories, I always remember the detail on the whiteboards of each beat was always tiny writing and it really filled up every scene with what the drive was, what was going on, and that's for five characters.
然后当我们梳理那些剧情时，我一直记得白板上每个情节节点的细节都是密密麻麻的小字，它真的用剧情的推动力和发展填满了每一个场景，而且那是针对五个角色的。

00:08:57.029 --> 00:09:11.389
And we really focused on that way more than what was gonna be funny, or like a set PC Act break here or there would be on the board, but it really was we would break a little romantic comedy every week.
我们真正关注的远比什么好笑要多得多，白板上可能偶尔会有个主打桥段或广告时段的断点，但实际上我们每周都在创作一部微型浪漫喜剧。

00:09:11.389 --> 00:09:21.990
Well, it feels like you can always insert jokes into a solid structure that's emotionally compelling, but the reverse is like to jam heart into a joke filter, which is pretty difficult.
嗯，感觉你总是可以把笑话塞进一个情感引人入胜的坚实结构里，但反过来，要把真心塞进一个笑话的筛子里，那就相当困难了。

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:26.470
We really wouldn't worry about whether it was that funny when we were breaking the story.
我们在梳理剧情时，真的不会去担心它到底有多好笑。

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:32.230
I don't know if that's probably really strange and maybe it was alarming to you to come into that, but it was like, we were pretty serious, it's true.
我不知道这听起来是不是真的很奇怪，也许你刚接触时会觉得有点警惕，但我们确实相当严肃，这是真的。

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:38.950
We would have like these very elaborate lengthy outlines that were just the emotional beats of the scenes and then add jokes.
我们会写出那种非常详尽、长篇大论的大纲，里面只有场景的情感节拍，然后再往里面加笑话。

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:41.110
And you were very, very good at that, Joe.
而你非常非常擅长这个，乔。

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:42.750
So we were lucky to have you join there.
所以我们很幸运能让你加入进来。

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:50.269
And this episode could not have been a better example of just let's just go insane with some Barney jokes.
而这一集简直就是“让我们围绕巴尼的笑话彻底疯狂一把”的最佳范例。

00:09:50.269 --> 00:09:53.470
And this is really an all-timer in that category.
在这个类别里，这绝对是历史级别的经典。

00:09:53.470 --> 00:09:54.350
It's bonkers.
太疯狂了。

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:55.429
It's bonkers.
太疯狂了。

00:09:55.429 --> 00:09:56.429
It is fun.
真的很有趣。

00:09:56.429 --> 00:10:14.806
And I wrote you this little ditty to sing to you to you in New York City will be right back.
我为你写了这段小曲子，要在纽约市唱给你听，我们马上回来。

00:10:14.806 --> 00:10:19.799
And now back to the show.
现在回到节目中。

00:10:19.799 --> 00:10:29.023
It also feels, Craig, we've talked about like when you really centered Ted as the romantic hero, it's like you need like a palate cleanser a little bit.
克雷格，这也让人觉得，我们之前讨论过，当你真正把泰德塑造为浪漫男主角时，你就需要一点像清口解腻的东西。

00:10:29.023 --> 00:10:31.450
Not because it's like we're sick of Ted.
倒不是因为我们对泰德感到厌烦。

00:10:31.450 --> 00:10:41.159
It's more like that's one speed that we can do, but there's also like we we have these other, we have these other speeds that are like really funny and strange.
更像是那是我们能驾驭的一种节奏，但除此之外，我们还有其他的节奏，那些节奏真的非常搞笑又古怪。

00:10:41.159 --> 00:10:55.360
I mean, we can talk about like how Barney is like, this is one of his, it's like a really funny episode for Barney, but it's also like, he said some stuff that's just like, oh my God, dude, you're sort of a war criminal in this episode.
我是说，我们可以聊聊巴尼是怎么样的，这一集对巴尼来说非常搞笑，但同时他也说了一些话，让人觉得，天哪，老兄，你在这一集里简直有点像个战犯。

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:56.200
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:00.960
But it's, there's a lot of really funny stuff, but it's this is, yeah, we went big.
但这里面有很多真的很好笑的东西，而且这集，是的，我们玩大了。

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:02.720
This was us going 12 out of 10.
这就是我们把音量开到十二分。

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.320
And we definitely went 12 out of 10.
我们绝对是拉满到了十二分。

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:06.519
He's the scorpion who stings in this episode.
他在这一集里就像那只蜇人的蝎子。

00:11:06.519 --> 00:11:08.679
Like he is honoring his nature.
他这是在遵循自己的天性。

00:11:08.679 --> 00:11:13.480
Although I will ask you something and I'm getting into the weeds of this episode and we don't have to go super specific just yet.
不过我要问你一件事，我开始深入探讨这一集的细节了，其实我们现在还不用聊得那么具体。

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:21.360
But I remember there was kind of a thing that Craig and I talked about as we watched the first two seasons, Joe, where we're like, does Barney sleep with a lot of women?
但我记得当我和克雷格观看前两季时，曾讨论过一个问题，乔，那就是：巴尼真的跟很多女人上过床吗？

00:11:21.360 --> 00:11:24.120
Or does Barney talk about sleeping with a lot of women?
还是说巴尼只是在吹嘘自己跟很多女人上过床？

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:29.240
And there was kind of question of like, is he an actual Lothario or is he a great storyteller?
当时有个疑问，他到底是一个真正的花花公子，还是一个很会讲故事的人？

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:33.200
And in this episode, it's kind of like, he's an actual Lothario.
而在这一集里，感觉就像是，他真的是个花花公子。

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:38.120
